/* book Worked example 2: Q: An NDIR CO analyser transmits 70 % of the light (I/I₀ = 0.70) with a 2.0 % CO span gas and 80 % with the engine sample. Assuming Beer–Lambert behaviour, find the CO in the sample.
   1. From I/I₀ = e−acL, ln(I/I₀) is proportional to c.
   2. Span: ln 0.70 = −0.3567. Sample: ln 0.80 = −0.2231.
   3. c = 2.0 % × (−0.2231) ÷ (−0.3567) = 2.0 × 0.6255 = 1.25 %.
   4. A straight-line guess from the transmissions, 2.0 × (1 − 0.80)/(1 − 0.70) = 1.33 %, would be 6 % high: the response is not linear.
   Answer: The sample holds 1.25 % CO. Real analysers store a curve made from several span gases for the same reason. */
